---
// Case: Saunter, Robert's own walking-pad app. Story from Robert (26.10.2026), facts from the public README and
// git log (~/Desktop/Job/laufband-public) and befund/saunter/case-study.md; numbers marked [prüfen] there are left
// out. The dashboard is rendered from the public copy (befund/saunter/overview.png).
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { full } from '../../lib/widths';
import CaseLayout from '../../components/case/CaseLayout.astro';
import dashboard from '../../assets/cases/saunter/dashboard.jpg';
import SaunterProtocol from '../../components/case/diagrams/SaunterProtocol.astro';
import SaunterSystem from '../../components/case/diagrams/SaunterSystem.astro';
import ScrubFigure from '../../components/stage/ScrubFigure.astro';

/** The film opens the page as a stage across the width (version J, live since 27 October 2026). */
const { stage = true } = Astro.props as { stage?: boolean };
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Saunter"
  years="2026"
  summary="I was tired of the remote and wanted a record of every office walk, so I built the app myself."
  meta={[
    ['Role', 'Problem, interaction design, decisions, testing on the real pad'],
    ['Built with', 'Claude Code as pair programmer'],
    ['Client', 'Own project'],
    ['Status', 'Open source (GPL-3.0)'],
  ]}
  links={[{ href: 'https://github.com/RobStraubm/saunter', label: 'Code on GitHub' }]}
  film="saunter"
  stage={stage ? 'saunter' : undefined}
  next={{ href: '/work/colla-parte/', title: 'Colla parte' }}
>
  <section>
    <h2>The itch</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">To change the speed I had to find a remote and press plus five times. Nothing was recorded.</p>
    <p>
      I walk on a small walking pad under my desk while I work. It came with a remote and a phone app, and neither kept
      a distance, a history or anything in Apple Health. I wanted the belt where my work already is, on the Mac, and a
      quiet record of every walk.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I built</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">A menu bar app that replaces the remote and turns walking into a habit.</p>
    <p>
      Start, pause and speed live in the menu bar and on keyboard shortcuts that work everywhere. Every walk is saved,
      and a finished walk goes to Apple Health, where it shows up in Fitness. On top sit a daily and a weekly streak and a
      virtual journey around Lake Constance from Konstanz on real footpaths, one walk at a time.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image
        src={dashboard}
        alt="Saunter overview: distance today, this week and in total, the journey around Lake Constance, the weekly streak at two of three walking days, distance over 30 days and the daily streak"
        widths={full([1000, 2000], dashboard)}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>The overview: distance, the journey around the lake, the weekly and the daily streak. German UI.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Friction log</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">I was the first user. Three things annoyed me, and each one changed the product.</p>
    <div class="c-trio">
      <div>
        <p><strong>The app kept asking where I had been.</strong></p>
        <p>
          The streak only counts office days, so after every weekday without a walk a banner asked whether I had been
          in the office. Now one question covers all open days, at most once a day, the last four weeks can be
          corrected in one list, and the app can recognise the office network on its own.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p><strong>The weekly goal was a lake.</strong></p>
        <p>
          At first the goal was one lap of Lake Constance a week, 204 km. On a Friday the app said I needed 67.6 km a
          day; that week I had walked 1.26 km. The goal is now a weekly streak: as many walking days as office days,
          minus one cheat day. The lake stayed, as the long journey.
        </p>
        {
          stage && (
            <figure>
              <ScrubFigure kind="saunter-lake" ratio="1 / 1" />
              <figcaption>The lap the goal asked for, the week I actually walked, the goal that replaced it.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          )
        }
      </div>
      <div>
        <p><strong>Bluetooth hung, and I said no to a watchdog.</strong></p>
        <p>
          After a few days the connection could get stuck while the pad was on and in range. Instead of a watchdog in
          the background there is a “search again” button that only appears while the pad is disconnected. No extra
          background activity on the machine I work on all day.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">One small daemon holds the connection, and everything else talks to it.</p>
    <p>
      A Python daemon on the Mac keeps the Bluetooth LE connection to the pad, records every session in SQLite and
      offers a local HTTP and WebSocket interface on 127.0.0.1. The SwiftUI menu bar app with its widget, Shortcuts and
      Siri, the terminal and Claude through an MCP server all talk to it. Data stays on the Mac. The pad speaks a short
      binary protocol: “set the belt to 2.0 km/h” is seven bytes.
    </p>
    <div class="c-pair">
      <figure>
        <SaunterSystem />
        <figcaption>One daemon, many ways in.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <SaunterProtocol />
        <figcaption>A real command, byte by byte, from a session on 23 October 2026.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p>
      The streak rules and the packet parsing are covered by unit tests that run on every push on GitHub Actions, which
      also builds the app. A first-launch setup finds the pad and asks for the few settings it needs, so nobody has to
      edit a config file.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Built with Claude Code</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">My part was the problem, the interaction design, every decision above and testing on the real pad.</p>
    <p>
      The code was written with Claude Code as pair programmer. For me that is the point of the project: a daily
      annoyance became a tool I use, and I stay responsible for what it does and how it feels.
    </p>
    <ol class="c-timeline">
      <li><time>Sep 2026</time><span>First version on my own pad</span></li>
      <li><time>29 Sep – 22 Oct</time><span>Friction log from daily use</span></li>
      <li><time>21 Oct</time><span>Public on GitHub</span></li>
      <li><time>23 Oct</time><span>Named Saunter, weekly streak, setup in the app, tests and CI</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Result</h2>
    <dl class="c-numbers">
      <div>
        <dt>0.71 km</dt>
        <dd>in 21 minutes at 2.0 km/h, one real walk</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>4</dt>
        <dd>ways to run the pad: menu bar, Shortcuts and Siri, terminal, Claude</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>2 days</dt>
        <dd>from the first public commit to tests and CI on every push</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Saunter runs my own walking pad, every finished walk goes to Apple Health, and the code is open source for anyone
      with the same kind of pad.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
